{
  "id": "T-296",
  "title": "Self tasks (tasks that change this repository itself) that change different files run at the same time: an open pull request is compared by the files it changes, not by its declared scope",
  "public_title": "Compare an open pull request by its changed files in the overlap check",
  "milestone": "M2",
  "depends_on": [],
  "scope": [
    "bin/lib/fm_stack.py",
    "bin/lib/fm_concurrent.py",
    "bin/lib/fm-stack.sh",
    "tests/**",
    "design/design.md",
    "design/tasks/T-296.json"
  ],
  "acceptance": [
    "Why. Self tasks (tasks that change this repository itself) that would change different files cannot be built at the same time. The overlap check compares the scope globs that specs declare, and nearly every spec declares `tests/**` and many declare `bin/lib/**`, so any two tasks look like they overlap. On 2026-10-11 the task that changes the retrospective's CI count and its tests (T-290) was held with \"fm-stack: overlaps T-287 (PR #274)\", although the pull request of T-287 (PR #274) changes only the Claude adapter, a few tests and design.md. The captain asked for independent tasks to be built in parallel.",
    "What, open pull request. When an in-flight task has an open pull request, the overlap check compares the files that pull request changes against its base with the scope of the candidate (the task that dispatch is about to start), instead of the in-flight task's declared scope.",
    "What, overlap. A candidate overlaps such a task only when at least one changed file of that pull request matches an entry of the candidate's scope. Files and scope entries under `tests/` never count as overlap, on either side and in both comparisons (changed files and declared scope): a shared test file is reconciled when the later branch catches up with its base, like any other base change.",
    "What, reading changes. The overlap check reads an open pull request's changed files from GitHub only when the candidate's scope overlaps that task's declared scope (the scope gate already keeps a pull request's changes inside its declared scope), at the first dispatch check and in `fm_stack select`. The check under the dispatch lock makes no GitHub call: it reuses the changed files read at the first check for the same pull request number and compares any other in-flight task by its declared scope. In a checkout where the GitHub repository cannot be resolved, those changed files count as unreadable.",
    "What, no pull request. An in-flight task with no pull request yet, or a reserved task (one picked earlier in the same dispatch run), is still compared by its declared scope, as today, except that entries under `tests/` never count.",
    "What, unknown changes. When the changed files of an open pull request cannot be read, the check holds the candidate under every stacking policy with the reason `overlap check unavailable: <task>`, the message used today for an unreadable scope; it never treats an unreadable pull request as no overlap and never stacks on it.",
    "What, later pushes. When an in-flight pull request later changes a file in the scope of a task already started beside it, nothing re-checks the pair; the conflict surfaces when the later branch catches up with its base and is resolved there.",
    "In flight. Candidates held under the old rule are re-checked on their next dispatch attempt under the new rule; tasks already stacked on an overlapped pull request keep their base; no event, pin or hold record is rewritten.",
    "What, unchanged. What happens after an overlap is found (hold, or stacking: basing the new branch on the single overlapped pull request's branch instead of `main`, and their messages) is unchanged.",
    "What, refused dispatch. A worker launch that the overlap check refuses leaves the task not in flight: its dispatched record no longer counts, so two refused tasks never hold each other (on 2026-10-11 T-290 and T-297 held each other until firstmate closed T-290 by hand).",
    "Done, tests. Tests show: a candidate with `tests/**` and one narrow code path is not held by an open pull request that changes other code files and one test file (`overlap: pr files decide`, fails on the base); a candidate with no pull request is not held by an in-flight task with no pull request when their declared scopes share only entries under `tests/` (`overlap: tests never count`, fails on the base); a candidate is held when the open pull request changes a file in its scope (`overlap: shared changed file holds`); a task with no pull request is still compared by scope (`overlap: no pr uses scope`); an unreadable pull request holds with `overlap check unavailable` (`overlap: unreadable pr holds`). The existing overlap tests keep their names and keep passing, except that `test_one_overlap_without_policy_holds_with_reason` now expects one changed-files read for the overlapping pull request instead of no GitHub read; two tasks refused by the overlap check do not hold each other afterwards (`overlap: refused dispatch is not in flight`, fail-first).",
    "Docs. `design/design.md` §15.8, the subsection that describes the self-task overlap check (T-278), says that an open pull request is compared by its changed files and that files and scope entries under `tests/` never count; it rewrites the sentence on when dispatch checks overlap \"without a GitHub call\" so that it names which checks read GitHub, and the sentence on when `select` resolves the GitHub repository."
  ],
